 Setting `SOMA_CORE_COUNTS = None` uses every mapped core (all cores in `parameters.act`). The per-variant
 parameters are in `SOMA_POPULATIONS`. Knob: `SOMA_TIMESTEPS`.
 
+### handshake
+
+Silent core pairs and intra-tile four-core cliques calibrate the incremental
+`energy_sync.per_message_received` cost and the fixed table costs
+`energy_sync.per_sending_core` and `energy_sync.per_receiving_core`. One neuron
+remains on every core while the number and shape of connected groups vary.
+Neuron count and occupancy are fixed; no spikes, axon scans, or inter-tile hops
+contribute. Two tick counts cancel programming and startup energy before fitting
+energy against nonempty fan-in/fan-out tables and directed core links:
+
+```sh
+python -m benchmarks handshake --variant lif --mode performance -j 4
+```
+
+For each topology, the energy slope `(E(24) - E(12)) / 12` removes fixed
+programming and startup costs. Subtract the unconnected slope, then fit
+`extra_energy_per_tick = tables * table_cost + links * link_cost`.
+A pair contributes two tables and one directed link; a four-core clique
+contributes eight tables and twelve directed links. Pair-only measurements
+cannot separate the two coefficients. Five pair counts
+(0, 1, 4, 16, 32) and three clique counts (1, 4, 16) produce sixteen runs on
+the 64-core chip when measured at both 12 and 24 ticks.
+
+Reciprocal groups of two and three cores add four latency-calibration runs.
+Their critical cores have two or four incident links, compared with six in a
+four-core clique. Fitting critical-core latency against incident degree, after
+subtracting the source chip's `latency_timestep_fixed`, estimates local table
+and link processing costs. These populate matching sender/receiver fields in
+`latency_sync`. Energy remains fitted using the original pairs and cliques.
+
+The chip synchronization model charges each nonempty fan-out table at its
+sending core, each nonempty fan-in table at its receiving core, and each directed
+link once at its receiver. Both table coefficients use the same calibrated cost.
+The chip-wide baseline is `energy_timestep_fixed`; all synchronization energy is
+reported in the `sync` category.
+Higher-degree sharing and routing remain approximate; validate dense topologies
+separately. `latency_sync` and `energy_sync` use the same endpoint field names,
+but their coefficients are calibrated independently. Fixed timestep costs use
+`latency_timestep_fixed` and `energy_timestep_fixed` are scalars. Optional
+`latency_sync.per_tile` and `energy_sync.per_tile` accept scalar,
+list or map values for an additional chip-wide size-dependent sync cost.
+
